00:00:00.360 --> 00:00:08.760
This man you see on the screen, Recep Tayyip&nbsp;
Erdoğan, has been lord and master of Turkish&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:08.760 --> 00:00:13.440
politics for the last 20 years. And now, to the&nbsp;
chagrin of virtually the entire Western bloc,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:13.440 --> 00:00:17.760
he has won at the polls again.
 
After surviving an obscure and bizarre&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.760 --> 00:00:22.140
coup d'état, ramming through the Constitution and&nbsp;
implementing a bizarre economic policy that has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.140 --> 00:00:27.360
hit the country hard and wrecked the value of&nbsp;
the Lira, Turks are still betting on Erdoğan.
 &nbsp;

00:00:27.360 --> 00:00:30.960
Under his leadership, Turkey has gone from&nbsp;
being a tolerant Islamic democracy and an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.960 --> 00:00:34.260
example of moderation to becoming an&nbsp;
increasingly uncomfortable partner for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.260 --> 00:00:38.040
both the European Union and NATO.
 
In fact, many consider that we are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.040 --> 00:00:42.360
on the verge of a new autocracy right at the&nbsp;
gates of Europe. For others, we are witnessing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.360 --> 00:00:46.080
Turkey's definitive break with the West.
 
Be that as it may, we are talking about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.080 --> 00:00:49.740
a candidate who has been seen handing&nbsp;
out wads of money at the doors of his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.740 --> 00:00:51.540
polling station on election day itself. You&nbsp;
don’t believe me? Well, see for yourselves.
 &nbsp;

00:00:51.540 --> 00:00:55.800
But how did we get here and what does it&nbsp;
all mean? How did this Turkish neo-sultan&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.800 --> 00:00:59.820
come into being? What is he after? Why did&nbsp;
he still manage to win the latest election?
 &nbsp;

00:00:59.820 --> 00:01:03.600
Well..., we are going to answer all these&nbsp;
questions, but first, let's get an overview&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.600 --> 00:01:06.960
of the situation.
 
Listen up.&nbsp;

00:01:14.100 --> 00:01:21.480
The Ottoman Empire participated in&nbsp;
World War One along with Germany,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:21.480 --> 00:01:25.860
the Austro-Hungarian Empire and Bulgaria, in what&nbsp;
would be the last great action of this Empire,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.860 --> 00:01:30.060
at least under that same denomination.
 
In 1918 they were on the losing side,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.060 --> 00:01:35.040
so the victorious Allies were able to force&nbsp;
the then Sultan Mehmet the Sixth to sign the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.040 --> 00:01:38.700
Treaty of Sèvres . This treaty confirmed the&nbsp;
surrender of the Ottoman Empire and meant the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.700 --> 00:01:42.420
division of its territories outside Turkey.
 
It also provided for international control&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.420 --> 00:01:45.780
of the city of Istanbul and the&nbsp;
Bosphorus and Dardanelles straits,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.780 --> 00:01:49.920
the creation of a large new independent&nbsp;
Armenian nation in eastern Anatolia, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.920 --> 00:01:54.360
a buffer zone for the Kurds, who would be free to&nbsp;
choose independence after a period of autonomy.
 &nbsp;

00:01:54.360 --> 00:01:58.020
However, this treaty was never ratified&nbsp;
by the Ottoman government due to Turkish&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.020 --> 00:02:03.540
resistance led by Mustafa Kemal Ataturk, who&nbsp;
led the so-called Turkish War of Independence.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.440 --> 00:02:12.120
As a result of this war, the Treaty of Sèvres&nbsp;
was replaced by the Treaty of Lausanne in 1923,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.120 --> 00:02:15.900
which established the modern borders of the&nbsp;
Republic of Turkey and annulled many of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.900 --> 00:02:19.260
provisions of the Treaty of Sèvres.
 
Later, the Second World War was already&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.260 --> 00:02:23.640
a clear sign of what would become, from then&nbsp;
on, the double face of Turkish foreign policy.&nbsp;

00:02:23.640 --> 00:02:27.180
You see, while the country signed a protection&nbsp;
agreement with France and the United Kingdom in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.180 --> 00:02:32.280
1939, the successes of the German blitzkriegled&nbsp;
Ankara to also sign a friendship treaty with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.280 --> 00:02:37.320
the Third Reich around 1941. The result? The&nbsp;
Turkish armed forces ended up being equipped&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.320 --> 00:02:42.480
with both Allied and Axis technology.
 
But yes, in 1945, the Turks definitely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.480 --> 00:02:45.180
went over to the Allies. It was&nbsp;
not a particularly loyal position,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.180 --> 00:02:50.160
but the advent of the Cold War meant that all&nbsp;
this back and forth was forgotten overnight.
 &nbsp;

00:02:50.160 --> 00:02:56.940
Competition with the Soviets prompted the&nbsp;
White House to step up to the plate and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.940 --> 00:03:00.720
sign a comprehensive security treaty with&nbsp;
Turkey, which has resulted in the delivery&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.720 --> 00:03:08.160
of large-scale military aid since 1947.
 
And so, this is how the special relationship&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.160 --> 00:03:11.400
between Washington and Ankara, and by&nbsp;
extension Turkey's relationship with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.400 --> 00:03:15.300
the entire Western world, came about. Thus,&nbsp;
the Turkish government signed its integration&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.300 --> 00:03:22.020
with the West, joining the Council of Europe&nbsp;
in 1949, NATO in 1952, and the OECD in 1961.
 &nbsp;

00:03:22.020 --> 00:03:27.060
However, Turkey was a country full of tensions:&nbsp;
externally, the deployment of US Jupiter nuclear&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:27.060 --> 00:03:31.740
missiles on its territory, aimed directly at the&nbsp;
Soviet Union, put the country at the epicenter&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:31.740 --> 00:03:36.300
of political debate throughout the Cold War.
 
On the other hand, internally, many politicians,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.300 --> 00:03:40.020
little by little, were trying to implement&nbsp;
a much more religious model for the country.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.020 --> 00:03:44.580
This was only stopped by the pressure of the&nbsp;
armed forces. For decades, the Turkish army&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.580 --> 00:03:47.880
was something of a shield against the&nbsp;
growing Islamization of the country.
 &nbsp;

00:03:47.880 --> 00:03:51.480
Although even this “mechanism” did not&nbsp;
always work in the most desirable way.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.480 --> 00:03:56.400
We are talking about two coups d'état,&nbsp;
one in 1960 and the other in 1980.

00:03:59.520 --> 00:04:04.320
And that is not all. For decades, the country was&nbsp;
also immersed in a great tug of war between State&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.320 --> 00:04:09.240
planning and market capitalism. Essentially,&nbsp;
between the end of World War Two and the 1980s,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.240 --> 00:04:13.620
planning policies and control of the economy&nbsp;
predominated. So did protectionism to supposedly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:13.620 --> 00:04:17.400
support domestic industries. It was only&nbsp;
from the 1980s onwards that the country&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:17.400 --> 00:04:25.560
turned to the open market and capitalism.
 
And, VisualPolitik viewers, let there be no&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:25.560 --> 00:04:30.420
doubt about it, Turkey's geographical position&nbsp;
as a nexus, a point of union between Asia,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.420 --> 00:04:34.260
Europe and the Middle East, was absolutely&nbsp;
crucial to this whole process. For this reason,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:34.260 --> 00:04:37.680
Western institutions tried to integrate&nbsp;
Turkey more and more. For a time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.680 --> 00:04:41.100
it even seemed reasonably close to&nbsp;
joining the European Union itself.
 &nbsp;

00:04:41.100 --> 00:04:44.040
Well, that was basically the country where&nbsp;
Recep Tayyip Erdoğan grew up and evolved,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:44.040 --> 00:04:47.880
a country halfway between East and West,&nbsp;
between secularization and laicization,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:47.880 --> 00:04:51.840
between capitalism and economic planning.
 
And so it was until the coming to power of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:51.840 --> 00:04:55.860
Erdoğan himself, the man who has practically&nbsp;
re-founded the country during the first two&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:55.860 --> 00:04:58.020
decades of the 21st century.
 
Listen up.
