the same year, and ended with dozens of people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:09.360 --> 00:13:13.680
injured, numerous journalists arrested, and strong&nbsp;
threats against the media. And not only that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:13.680 --> 00:13:17.640
during this period, Erdoğan's government tried&nbsp;
different kinds of ways to censor the internet.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:17.640 --> 00:13:24.840
For example, it restricted the use of Twitter:
 
(26 June 2014: Internet censorship in Turkey.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:24.840 --> 00:13:30.540
Since the Taksim Square protests began in the&nbsp;
summer of 2013, the Turkish government has been&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:30.540 --> 00:13:36.000
stepping up violence to crack down on them. A&nbsp;
new law allows it to shut down blogs and Twitter&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:36.000 --> 00:13:40.680
accounts that are inconvenient to it. – DW)
 
Likewise, Erdoğan gradually redesigned the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:40.680 --> 00:13:44.400
institutions to his liking and since&nbsp;
2010 prosecutors close to his party&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:44.400 --> 00:13:49.080
began a campaign of arrests and interrogations&nbsp;
against senior officers of the armed forces.
 &nbsp;

00:13:49.080 --> 00:13:57.180
And so, on 15 July 2016, an attempted coup&nbsp;
took place. For many it was the sign of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:57.180 --> 00:14:01.680
military's weariness, for others a self-coup&nbsp;
organized by Erdoğan himself to clean house.
 &nbsp;

00:14:01.680 --> 00:14:07.620
Whatever the case, the coup that resulted in&nbsp;
194 dead and more than 1,100 wounded failed and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:07.620 --> 00:14:12.240
this political leader took total control. Let's&nbsp;
just say that the coup gave him the possibility&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:12.240 --> 00:14:16.380
to overhaul the army from top to bottom.
 
Already without the counterweight of the army,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:16.380 --> 00:14:20.640
in 2017 Erdoğan promoted a constitutional&nbsp;
reform that gave the ruler the power to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:20.640 --> 00:14:23.820
dissolve parliament and even appoint&nbsp;
half of the Constitutional Court.
 &nbsp;

00:14:23.820 --> 00:14:27.000
From that moment on, all indicators&nbsp;
of freedom have fallen in the country,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:27.000 --> 00:14:30.480
the persecution of opponents has become&nbsp;
frequent and the media has been gradually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:30.480 --> 00:14:34.920
tamed, if not controlled in most cases.
 
To make matters worse, from that moment on,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:34.920 --> 00:14:39.180
foreign investment began to plummet and Erdoğan&nbsp;
brought to the table an economic policy based&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:39.180 --> 00:14:44.400
on Islamic dogmas, For instance, against interest&nbsp;
rates, which has destroyed the value of the lira.
 &nbsp;

00:14:44.400 --> 00:14:48.240
In a way, Turkey has become more and more like&nbsp;
Russia... A country with which, despite being&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:48.240 --> 00:14:53.100
a NATO member, it maintains practically full&nbsp;
relations. Do you want proof? Just take a look:

00:14:53.100 --> 00:14:58.620
(27 April 2023: Erdoğan and&nbsp;
Putin celebrate cooperation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:58.620 --> 00:15:01.020
on Turkey's first nuclear power plant. – Euronews)

00:15:01.020 --> 00:15:05.100
Nevertheless, despite all this,&nbsp;
Erdoğan has won the elections again.
 &nbsp;

00:15:05.100 --> 00:15:11.700
Listen up.
 
(A POLITICAL EARTHQUAKE)
 &nbsp;

00:15:15.600 --> 00:15:19.500
In February of this year, the Turkish city&nbsp;
of Gaziantep suffered a terrible earthquake,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:19.500 --> 00:15:23.520
which killed more than 55,000 people. The&nbsp;
lack of assistance and the government's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:23.520 --> 00:15:27.360
disastrous handling of the tragedy seemed&nbsp;
to open the door to political change.

00:15:27.360 --> 00:15:34.620
(The economy is in ruins. AKP – Erdogan's&nbsp;
party – has depleted reserves and has vastly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:34.620 --> 00:15:39.300
expanded pledges and spending to bolster&nbsp;
its patronage network. I don't know how&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:39.300 --> 00:15:44.820
the country can avoid economic collapse. –&nbsp;
Daron Acemoglu, economist and MIT professor)
 &nbsp;

00:15:44.820 --> 00:15:47.940
What’s more, most of the opposition&nbsp;
united under a single candidacy:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:47.940 --> 00:15:52.380
Kemal Kılıçdaroğlu [keˈmal kutzdaɾolu], who&nbsp;
became the big favorite... And yet he lost.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:52.380 --> 00:15:56.940
He lost in the first and second rounds.
 
The Turks have bet on a political leader&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:56.940 --> 00:16:00.540
who has shredded civil liberties, provoked&nbsp;
an economic crisis and who has clashed with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:00.540 --> 00:16:04.080
NATO and the European Union. And all&nbsp;
apparently for one very clear premise:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:04.080 --> 00:16:08.340
to make Turkey a great Islamic power.&nbsp;
Erdoğan has nurtured an unbridled nationalist&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:08.340 --> 00:16:12.120
sentiment that has now given him victory.
 
We could say that he has become a kind of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:12.120 --> 00:16:16.600
Putin in Turkey. Once a moderate politician with&nbsp;
a zeal for renewing ties with the West, today he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:16.600 --> 00:16:21.660
has become almost, almost, a kind of nightmare.&nbsp;
An ally more uncomfortable than many enemies.

00:16:21.660 --> 00:16:23.580
The question is:
 
How far do you think&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:23.580 --> 00:16:26.460
Erdoğan will go? Will Turkey continue&nbsp;
to fall down the slippery slope of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:26.460 --> 00:16:29.820
autocracy? Will this country become&nbsp;
a kind of Russia in the Middle East?

